papers.abstract:
We consider simple random walk on the sequential affine preferential attachment graphs with fixed parameters $m\ge2$ and $\delta\ge0$. We prove the walk has total-variation cutoff, and for every fixed $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$, \[ t_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(n)}(\varepsilon) =\frac{\log n}{\nu d}
  +O_{\mathbb P}\!\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right), \] where $\nu>0$ is the walk speed and $d>0$ is the entropy per level of harmonic measure on the P\'olya--point tree, the local weak limit of the graph. Thus cutoff occurs at the entropic time.
  Entropic cutoff was previously established for a range of random graph models including configuration models under mild degree assumptions and on Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi random graphs for typical starting points. We extend the harmonic-measure approach to preferential attachment, accounting for its age-dependent structure and hubs of polynomial degree. The local weak limit, the P\'olya--point tree, provides a more tractable setting for analyzing the walk. The proof combines regeneration estimates on the local limit tree, a quantitative graph--tree coupling on the mixing-time scale, and control of hub excursions. We show that shortly before the cutoff time its distribution is close in total variation distance to a measure with small $L^\infty$ distance from stationarity. When $m\ge2$, the spectral gap is bounded away from zero with high probability which completes the upper-bound coupling argument.

Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb R^n$, $n\ge3$, be a bounded uniformly convex $C^{3,\gamma}$ domain, where $0<\gamma<1$. We study the limit as the positive Robin parameter $\alpha\to\infty$. For the $2$-Hessian torsion problem, let $U$ be the admissible Dirichlet solution. For all sufficiently large $\alpha$, we obtain a unique admissible Robin solution $u_\alpha$ and establish the optimal-order estimate \[
  \|u_\alpha-U\|_{C^{2,\gamma}(\overline\Omega)}
  \le C\alpha^{-1}. \] Moreover, $-\sqrt{-u_\alpha}$ has a uniform positive lower bound on its Hessian, and hence $\sqrt{-u_\alpha}$ is strictly concave. For the normalized eigenvalue problem, let $(\lambda_D,\varphi_D)$ be the normalized negative admissible Dirichlet eigenpair, and assume $\varphi_D\in C^{2,\gamma}(\overline\Omega)$. For all sufficiently large $\alpha$, there is a unique normalized negative admissible Robin eigenpair $(\lambda_\alpha,\varphi_\alpha)$, which satisfies \[
  \|\varphi_\alpha-\varphi_D\|_{C^{2,\gamma}(\overline\Omega)}
  +|\lambda_\alpha-\lambda_D|\le C\alpha^{-1}. \] The Hessian of $-\log(-\varphi_\alpha)$ also has a uniform positive lower bound. The main analytic tool is a uniform H\"older estimate for linear Robin problems, with an $\alpha^{-1}$ gain for $C^{2,\gamma}$ boundary data. A normalized augmented inverse removes the scaling kernel in the eigenvalue problem. A Schur-complement argument combines the $C^2$ convergence with boundary geometry to prove convexity up to the boundary and anisotropic Hessian lower bounds at the scale ${\rm dist}(x,\partial\Omega)+\alpha^{-1}$. The convergence rate $O(\alpha^{-1})$ for the Robin solution functions is optimal even in $C^0$, as follows from their boundary conditions.

For a simple graph $G$ with $n$ vertices, its $w$-polynomial is defined by \begin{align*}
  \sum_{q\geq0}P(G;q)x^q
  =\frac{w(G;x)}{(1-x)^{n+1}}, \end{align*} where $P(G;x)$ is the chromatic polynomial of $G$. This polynomial was introduced by Brenti in 1992. In this paper, we prove Conjecture~6.7 and the $w$-assertion of Conjecture~6.5 posed by Brenti, Royle and Wagner in 1994. The first states that $w(C_n;x)$ has only real zeros for $n\ge 5$. The second assertion states that if $G\cap H$ is complete and both $w(G;x)$ and $w(H;x)$ have only real zeros, then $w(G\cup H;x)$ has only real zeros.

We study attainment of the best constant in \begin{equation*}
  \int_{\mathbb R^{m+n}}
  \bigl(|\nabla_xu|^2+|x|^2|\nabla_yu|^2+a|u|^2\bigr)
  \geq S_a\left(\int_{\mathbb R^{m+n}}
  (1+|x|^2)^{n/(Q-2)}|u|^q\right)^{2/q}, \end{equation*} where \(m,n\geq1\), \(Q=m+2n\), \(q=2Q/(Q-2)\), and \(a>0\). Using annular interpolation and a transverse-average recurrence with \(L^q\) contraction \(2^{-m/q}\), we decompose bounded sequences into finite, Grushin and Euclidean profiles. Energy and critical mass split, with a power bound in the number of profiles on the remaining mass. We prove compactness of normalized minimizing sequences modulo \(y\)-translations under \(S_a<\min\{S_G,a^{n/Q}S_E(1)\}\), where \(S_G\) and \(S_E(1)\) are the tangent constants. The strict-binding set is empty or an open interval; we prove nonemptiness for \(n=1\) and even \(m\geq6\). At finite positive endpoints of a nonempty binding interval, we characterize attainment by first-order binding gaps. We obtain a finite critical-profile decomposition of Palais--Smale sequences with a remainder vanishing in energy.

Let \(s>0\), \(\alpha,\gamma\in\mathbb{R}\), and let \(\psi\colon \mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{R}_{>0}\) be a non-increasing function. Consider the limsup set \[
  W_s(\psi,\alpha,\gamma) = \left\{
  \beta\in[0,1] \colon
  \norm{n\beta-\gamma} < \frac{\psi(n)}{\norm{n\alpha}^{\,s}}
  \ \text{ for infinitely many } n\in\mathbb{N}
  \right\}. \] Chow [Duke J. Math. 2018] settled the measure-theoretic result for \(s=1\). In this paper, we investigate the Lebesgue measure of \(W_s(\psi,\alpha,\gamma)\) for general \(s>0\).

We establish a dimension-free long-time reverse transportation, respectively Harnack inequality for the underdamped Langevin dynamics with non-convex potential. Specifically, we prove that for any two initial points $z,z'$ and $P_t$ the underdamped Langevin semigroup, it holds \begin{equation}
  \mathrm{KL}(\delta_z P_t|\delta_{z'}P_t)
  \leq C\mathrm{e}^{-\chi t}|z-z'|(\mathcal{R}\vee|z-z'|) \end{equation} for all $t>\delta$ where $\delta>0$ is arbitrarily small and $C=C(\delta),\mathcal{R},\chi>0$ are dimension free constants. In particular, our proof extends recent results which are restricted to either the convex underdamped or the non-convex but overdamped setting.

Given an integer $k \geq 2$, there are integers $M_k$ and $P_k$ so that for all $A \subseteq \mathbb F^n_p$ does not contain a restricted polynomial progression of the form
  \begin{align*}
  x, x+\begin{pmatrix}
  y_1 \\ y_2 \\ \vdots \\ y_n
  \end{pmatrix}, x+\begin{pmatrix}
  y_1^2 \\ y_2^2 \\ \vdots \\ y_n^2
  \end{pmatrix}, \ldots, x+\begin{pmatrix}
  y_1^{k-1} \\ y_2^{k-1} \\ \vdots \\ y_n^{k-1}
  \end{pmatrix},
  \end{align*}
  where $x \in \mathbb F^n_p$, with $p>P_k$, and $y \in \{0,2, 3, \ldots, M_k\}^n \backslash \{\vec{0} \}$, for some fixed $M_k$, then $A$ must have density bounded above by $\frac{C}{(\log^{(k^{O(k)})} n)^c}$, where $C, c>0$ only depend on $k$ and $p$. For $k\geq 4$, the best previously known bound came from the density Hales--Jewett theorem and was of inverse Ackermann type. The proof uses a recent inverse theorem for noise stability of Bhangale, Khot, Liu and Minzer, which arose in the study of constraint satisfaction problems. The main new ingredient is a proof that the underlying distribution counting restricted polynomial progressions does not admit a nontrivial abelian embedding, by way of an involved algebraic argument. Reasonable bounds are provided for $P_k$ and $M_k$.
